WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.482
Express applications rely heavily on npm
dependencies and middleware packages.

2
00:00:08.562 --> 00:00:15.733
Every package that parses input, handles
authentication, signs tokens,

3
00:00:15.813 --> 00:00:21.713
processes files, renders templates,
connects to services,

4
00:00:21.793 --> 00:00:29.080
or changes build behavior becomes part of
the application security story.

5
00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:37.373
Reviewing package.json, lockfile changes,
install scripts, package provenance,

6
00:00:37.453 --> 00:00:45.435
and security advisories helps teams
understand what is being added to the runtime

7
00:00:45.515 --> 00:00:47.481
and build pipeline.

8
00:00:47.561 --> 00:00:55.080
npm audit is useful input, but it is not a
complete security program by itself.

9
00:00:55.160 --> 00:01:01.869
Secrets should never be treated as
ordinary configuration strings.

10
00:01:01.949 --> 00:01:08.890
Database passwords, API keys, OAuth client
secrets, JWT signing keys,

11
00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:13.597
session secrets, webhook secrets, npm
tokens,

12
00:01:13.677 --> 00:01:18.535
and service credentials should not be
hardcoded,

13
00:01:18.615 --> 00:01:25.903
committed in real .env files, printed
during startup, copied into images,

14
00:01:25.983 --> 00:01:33.502
exposed in frontend bundles, stored in
tickets, or logged during debugging.

15
00:01:33.582 --> 00:01:39.134
Environment variables can deliver secrets
to a process,

16
00:01:39.214 --> 00:01:45.807
but they are not a full secrets-management
strategy by themselves.

17
00:01:45.887 --> 00:01:52.828
Configuration hygiene means separating
development, test, staging,

18
00:01:52.908 --> 00:01:56.609
and production values deliberately.

19
00:01:56.689 --> 00:02:04.787
A .env.example file can document required
settings without exposing real values.

20
00:02:04.867 --> 00:02:13.080
CI/CD variables and secret managers can
provide protected runtime configuration.

21
00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:18.134
Rotation should be possible without code
changes.

22
00:02:18.214 --> 00:02:25.848
Logs should avoid authorization headers,
cookies, session IDs, reset links,

23
00:02:25.928 --> 00:02:29.167
tokens, and sensitive payloads.

24
00:02:29.247 --> 00:02:37.460
Secure code can still fail badly when
dependencies or secrets are handled casually.
